so hi Steve here again Steve Kaufman I'm  in the interior British Columbia in  Osoyoos playing golf with friends a  lovely part of British Columbia I was on  the internet and someone talked about  the phonetic the international phonetic  alphabet and the need for some set of  symbols that would you know accurately  represent sounds in a language because  as we know especially in English the  alphabet doesn't really represent the  sounds very accurately because it you  know the letter O can be pronounced Oh  or a or whatever depending on whether  it's women or come or over so however I  don't think the international phonetic  alphabet is all that useful  I have never bothered to learn the  symbols of the IPA because to me I have  to first hear the language I have to  hear it accurately and as a non-native  speaker is often difficult to hear it's  difficult to hear where one word ends  and the next word begins and so forth so  one of the really useful tools that we  have today to teach ourselves to hear  the language accurately so that we can  then pronounce it is text-to-speech so  one of the things that I do which  unfortunately I can't do for Ukrainian  because there is no text-to-speech or  Google text-to-speech for Ukrainian  which is very unfortunate but it works  for Polish and for Russian extremely  well  and that is that I save phrases at link  and those phrases then I review them in  the dictation function we have and so I  hear the text to speech sounds and I  have to write it down and it's amazing  how much I miss because I don't  accurately hear what the native speaker  in this case that text-to-speech is  saying so it's a very good way to train  yourself to hear what the person is  actually saying so that's one thing that  I can do with text-to-speech now the  thing I've started doing with  text-to-speech but again only in Polish  or Russian where text-to-speech is  available is that I will have saved  these phrases in  text link and then I review them in  flashcards and we also have  text-to-speech in the flashcard so I  start up an audio of recording so I will  press the text-to-speech on here the  text-to-speech then I repeat it in my  own pronunciation then I go to the next  flashcard and do the same for the next  phrase and so forth and so on and pretty  soon I have a sound file of two minutes  long or so which consists of the  text-to-speech pronunciation of the  phrase and then my own pronunciation I  then listen to that sound file while  reviewing my flashcards and all of this  is helping me to notice what actually is  being said and I think a big part of  pronunciation is actually accurately  hearing what is said I mean we know for  example that often Japanese speakers  have trouble distinguishing between l  and r when they speak but they make the  same mistake when they write and that  suggests to me that they're not hearing  the L and the R when they listen to the  language so we have to train our ability  to hear there are even other ways in  which you can use text-to-speech to help  your pronunciation but I won't go in  though into those today but we are going  to be experimenting with some of those  with our the members of our you know  link Academy live and you should be  following their activities and I'll  leave a link to the videos about their  activities we're going to be working  with them on their pronunciation using  text-to-speech but we won't be using the  international phonetic alphabet  however for those people who find that  useful by all means the main thing in  language learning is to do what you like  to do and what you find useful so and  now I'm going out to play golf thank you  for listening  you
 

